WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.800
Film Courage: What are 5 things a writer should
know about the TV writing room?

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:10.200
Jeffrey Davis: You take 2 and a half and I’ll
take 2 and a half.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:11.200
Peter Desberg: [Laughs]

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:12.900
Jeffrey Davis: Go ahead…want me to start?

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:13.900
Peter Desberg: Yeah.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:14.900
Go ahead.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:19.500
Jeffrey Davis: Know that to get in you’ve
got to…90 percent chance you are going to

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:27.490
have to work your way into the room as script
assistant, as a writer’s assistant.

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:32.590
Those jobs are plentiful and great and you
will get training.

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:35.540
Know that it will take awhile.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:40.380
Also know that when you are a baby writer…don’t
talk, listen.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:47.050
No one likes someone who answers questions.

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:50.600
Know that people are good at one or two things
but no one is good at everything.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.340
I think I answered three that leaves you two.

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:54.020
Peter Desberg: And don’t be the smartest
person in the room.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:55.020
Ever, ever…

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:56.550
Jeffrey Davis: Never be the smartest person
in the room.

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:57.550
Film Courage: Why?

00:00:57.550 --> 00:00:59.400
Peter Desberg: Because everybody will hate
you…

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.520
Jeffrey Davis: Especially the show runner.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:06.040
Peter Desberg: And you won’t be the smartest
person in the room and everyone will see that.

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:09.750
And really, you cannot be full of yourself.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:14.030
Jeffrey Davis: I’m not going to mention
the show.

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:20.940
I will mention that it was my friend Bob who
was very close to as I said Chuck Lurie (who

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:22.700
gave Chuck Lurie his break).

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:27.950
And Chuck will call Bob every once in awhile
even though Bob is semi-retired and say “I

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:31.540
really need help” and it was a point a of
couple years ago when he had five shows on

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:32.540
the air.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:40.030
And he went on one of the shows and he pitched
a joke and he said to Bob “Was that funny?

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:44.600
When you were working on My Two Dads, was
that funny back then?

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:47.620
Or on Facts of Life?”

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:51.600
And Chuck went to Bob and said “Look, I
don’t need the money.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:52.850
I’m out of here.”

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:53.850
And he left.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:02:00.490
If you’re a show runner, the best show runner
you can be is the kind of person who mentors

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:01.870
other people.

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:08.729
Who is secure enough (like Phil Rosenthal)
to really care about getting the best out

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:09.729
of people.

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:10.729
That’s the best kind of leader.

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:12.080
Peter Desberg: We had [in Peter and Jeffrey’s
new book Now That’s Funny) a lot of great

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:15.010
stories about rooms and show runners.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:17.620
And Peter Casey was great.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:20.820
He said “I worked on The Jeffersons.

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:23.690
And I came to the room late one day.

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:25.300
And I hadn’t eaten breakfast.

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:28.560
And I brought a little box of Corn Flakes
with me.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:32.790
And I went to the corner where the coffee
was, poured it in and took a little of the

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:34.990
cream and poured it on there.

00:02:34.990 --> 00:02:36.770
And the guy said ‘That’s not for your
cereal.

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:38.110
That’s for the coffee!”

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:41.010
And he charged him like 25 cents for doing
that.

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:42.630
Film Courage: Oh…my!

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:47.160
Peter Desberg: And they would feed us the
worst food ever.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:52.000
He said when I went to work on Cheers with
The Charles Brothers, they would take us out

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:57.380
to really good restaurants or if they had
to work late they’d call up one of the really

00:02:57.380 --> 00:03:00.590
good restaurants and we’d order from the
menu and they would deliver it.

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:05.340
They said they treated the writers with so
much kindness and respect and they got such

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:06.340
great work out of us.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:08.780
Nobody complained if it was late.

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:14.000
Where with The Jeffersons everybody was grumbling
all the time and hated being in the room.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.300
So he said that The Charles Brothers made
us purr like kittens.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:19.380
Jeffrey Davis: I don’t think he actually
said that.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:21.910
Peter Desberg: Alright, I am taking license.

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:25.040
Film Courage: Was there another because I
think you only got one in?

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:27.300
Jeffrey Davis: No that’s four altogether.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:28.300
Film Courage: Four?

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:29.300
Five total…

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:30.910
Peter Desberg: Be careful!

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:33.190
Film Courage: Be careful.

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:34.190
Okay.

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:35.190
Know where the exit is?

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:40.900
Jeffrey Davis: This is sort of repetitious
but I think it bears repeating.

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:49.239
It’s really important that when you get
into a room you use it as a learning experience

00:03:49.239 --> 00:03:54.129
because you are one of the very few people.

00:03:54.129 --> 00:03:56.330
When I started there was still a freelance
market.

00:03:56.330 --> 00:04:02.400
There really is no freelance market anymore
and so if you have the luck to get into a

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:07.450
room take advantage of it and be humble and
Peter’s right…

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:13.480
Peter Desberg: But I will add a fifth just
for the sake of completeness that every room

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:14.480
has a culture…

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:15.480
Jeffrey Davis: Oh…that’s true.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:21.249
Peter Desberg: And you want to spend a lot
of time observing before you jump in and just

00:04:21.249 --> 00:04:25.949
because one person makes a joke doesn’t
mean everybody makes jokes and if one person

00:04:25.949 --> 00:04:31.629
kids another person that doesn’t mean that
everybody teases and you want to wait and

00:04:31.629 --> 00:04:36.310
when you really understand how people interact,
then you slowly test the waters and start

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:37.310
to do it.

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:38.889
Even then…you do it slowly.

00:04:38.889 --> 00:04:45.021
Jeffrey Davis: My two wonderful former students
who are on One Day at a Time were on the show

00:04:45.021 --> 00:04:47.490
(now they are on their second season).

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:49.559
They are not the baby writers anymore.

00:04:49.559 --> 00:04:51.460
You see how that works?

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:56.169
So they were on the show, they were on like
three months, they were on like show seven

00:04:56.169 --> 00:04:57.689
or eight.

00:04:57.689 --> 00:04:58.689
And they hadn’t said that much.

00:04:58.689 --> 00:05:02.691
I mean they’ve done their work, they were
the first script their mentor wrote with them

00:05:02.691 --> 00:05:09.309
(so it was the three of them writing it) which
was really generous of her (that doesn’t

00:05:09.309 --> 00:05:12.360
always happen).

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:19.270
And they didn’t say anything one day and
one of the writers said “Caroline, we’d

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:20.830
really like to hear what you guys think.

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:25.719
You guys are one of these character’s age.”

00:05:25.719 --> 00:05:31.689
Opening and yet she began by the “What if”
thing.

00:05:31.689 --> 00:05:35.969
So that left room to be wrong if they didn’t
like what she said.

00:05:35.969 --> 00:05:41.940
And after that day they were like “You guys
should…whenever you have something to say,

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:42.940
say it.

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:43.960
Don’t be shy.”

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:48.809
But they were smart to wait because they were
learning, they were observing and they were

00:05:48.809 --> 00:05:49.919
only 25-years-old.

00:05:49.919 --> 00:05:56.649
That’s young to be in a room.

00:05:56.649 --> 00:05:59.550
And they didn’t think the show was going
to get a second season.

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:04.159
And now it’s big heads, it’s Emmy-nominated
and it’s a big hit.

00:06:04.159 --> 00:06:06.379
Peter Desberg: By the way, you tricked us.

00:06:06.379 --> 00:06:08.819
You said only three more questions and the
first one is a five-parter.

00:06:08.819 --> 00:06:11.319
Don’t think that went past us.

00:06:11.319 --> 00:06:14.729
Jeffrey Davis: It went past me.

00:06:14.729 --> 00:06:15.729
You make her laugh.

00:06:15.729 --> 00:06:17.490
Peter Desberg: She has a low-threshold.

